import Foundation

struct SkillDescriptions {
    static let descriptions: [String: String] = [
        // Core Mindfulness
        "Wise Mind": "The synthesis of emotion mind and reasonable mind, where intuition and logic work together. Use this when you need to make important decisions or respond to situations skillfully. Practice by quieting yourself and asking what feels right when balancing both thoughts and feelings.",

        "Observe": "Noticing your inner and outer experiences without putting words on them. Use this to become aware of what's happening in the present moment without getting caught up in reactions. Practice by watching your breath, sensations, thoughts, or emotions like clouds passing by.",

        "Describe": "Putting words on what you observe, sticking to the facts without interpretations. Use this after observing to acknowledge experiences clearly and communicate them accurately. Practice by labeling sensations, thoughts, and emotions using neutral language like 'I notice tension in my shoulders' rather than 'my shoulders are killing me.'",

        "Participate": "Fully entering into experiences without self-consciousness, becoming one with your activity. Use this to engage completely in life rather than sitting on the sidelines. Practice by throwing yourself into activities like dancing, conversation, or work without worrying about how you look or perform.",

        "Nonjudgmental Stance": "Observing and describing without evaluating as good or bad, right or wrong. Use this to reduce suffering caused by judgments and see situations more clearly. Practice by replacing judgmental words with factual descriptions and catching when you label things as 'should' or 'shouldn't.'",

        "One-Mindfully": "Doing one thing at a time with complete attention, letting go of distractions. Use this to improve focus, effectiveness, and reduce anxiety from scattered attention. Practice by bringing your mind back to one task whenever it wanders, whether eating, talking, or working.",

        "Effectively": "Doing what works in the situation, focusing on outcomes rather than being 'right.' Use this when being righteous or rigid keeps you from your goals. Practice by asking 'what's the effective thing to do here?' and letting go of needing to prove yourself or punish others.",

        "Loving Kindness": "Cultivating an attitude of warmth, compassion, and goodwill toward yourself and others. Use this to soften harsh self-criticism and build connection with others. Practice by repeating phrases like 'may I/you be safe, may I/you be happy' while bringing to mind yourself, loved ones, neutral people, and difficult people.",

        "Balancing Doing Mind and Being Mind": "Shifting between goal-focused action mode and present-moment acceptance mode. Use this to avoid burnout from constant doing or stagnation from only being. Practice by scheduling both productive activities and moments of simply experiencing without goals.",

        "Walking the Middle Path": "Finding synthesis between opposite positions rather than getting stuck in extremes. Use this to resolve conflicts, reduce black-and-white thinking, and validate multiple truths. Practice by looking for 'both-and' instead of 'either-or' and finding what's true in opposing viewpoints.",

        "Pros and Cons of Practicing Mindfulness": "Weighing the benefits and costs of mindfulness practice to motivate yourself. Use this when resistance to mindfulness arises or you need to strengthen commitment. Practice by listing short-term and long-term advantages and disadvantages of practicing versus not practicing.",

        "Mindfulness of Pleasant Events": "Intentionally noticing and savoring positive experiences as they occur. Use this to build positive emotion, counteract negativity bias, and increase joy. Practice by focusing full attention on pleasant moments, noticing body sensations, thoughts, and feelings during positive events.",

        // Interpersonal Effectiveness
        "DEAR MAN": "A strategy for asking for what you want or saying no effectively. Describe the situation, Express feelings, Assert needs, Reinforce the other person, stay Mindful of goals, Appear confident, and Negotiate. Use this when you need to make requests, set boundaries, or resolve conflicts.",

        "GIVE": "A strategy for maintaining and strengthening relationships while making requests. Be Gentle in approach, act Interested in the other person, Validate their feelings, and use an Easy manner. Use this when the relationship is a priority and you want to keep respect while asking for something.",

        "FAST": "A strategy for keeping self-respect in interactions. Be Fair to yourself and others, no unnecessary Apologies, Stick to your values, and be Truthful. Use this when you're tempted to compromise your integrity or over-apologize to please others.",

        "Options for Intensity": "Choosing how strong or soft to be in asking or refusing based on the situation. Use this to calibrate your approach by considering factors like relationship importance, goals, rights, and power dynamics. Practice by assessing the situation before acting and adjusting intensity up or down as needed.",

        "Pros and Cons of IE Skills": "Weighing benefits and costs of using interpersonal skills versus avoiding or being passive. Use this to motivate yourself when anxiety makes you want to avoid important conversations. Practice by listing what you gain and lose from speaking up versus staying silent.",

        "Prioritizing Goals": "Deciding whether objectives, relationships, or self-respect matter most in a situation. Use this to guide which skills to emphasize in interactions. Practice by identifying your top priority before important conversations and choosing strategies that support that goal.",

        "Troubleshooting IE Skills": "Identifying why interpersonal skills aren't working and making adjustments. Use this when you've tried IE skills but aren't getting results. Practice by checking if you assessed intensity correctly, prepared adequately, or if myths and judgments are interfering.",

        "Finding and Getting People to Like You": "Building new relationships through proximity, similarity, and warmth. Use this to expand your social network and create new connections. Practice by putting yourself in situations with people who share interests, being friendly, and showing genuine interest in others.",

        "Ending Relationships": "Deciding when to end relationships and doing so skillfully. Use this when a relationship is harmful, one-sided, or no longer serving you. Practice by evaluating whether to end, reduce, or continue relationships and communicating decisions clearly and respectfully.",

        "Think and Act Dialectically": "Recognizing and balancing opposing truths in relationships. Use this to move past rigidity and see multiple valid perspectives. Practice by looking for what's being left out, finding middle ground, and validating different viewpoints simultaneously.",

        "Self-Validation": "Acknowledging your own feelings, thoughts, and experiences as valid and understandable. Use this to build self-respect and emotional stability. Practice by treating your reactions as reasonable given your history and current situation, without requiring external approval.",

        "Validating Others": "Communicating that another person's feelings and thoughts make sense. Use this to strengthen relationships, reduce conflict, and help others feel heard. Practice by listening for emotions, reflecting understanding, and showing that responses are reasonable given their experience.",

        "Changing Behavior with Reinforcement": "Using rewards and consequences to shape behavior in yourself and others. Use this to increase desired behaviors and decrease unwanted ones. Practice by clearly reinforcing wanted actions immediately and consistently while withholding reinforcement for problem behaviors.",

        // Emotion Regulation
        "Identifying Primary Emotions": "Distinguishing the first emotion that arises from secondary reactive emotions. Use this to understand what you're really feeling and respond effectively. Practice by noticing quick flashes of feeling before secondary reactions like anger over hurt or anxiety over sadness.",

        "Pros and Cons of Changing Emotions": "Weighing whether to accept or try to change an emotional response. Use this when deciding whether an emotion fits the situation or needs regulation. Practice by listing advantages and disadvantages of staying with the emotion versus working to shift it.",

        "Check the Facts": "Examining whether your emotional intensity matches the actual facts of the situation. Use this when emotions feel overwhelming or you're unsure if they fit. Practice by listing facts only, checking interpretations and assumptions, and asking if the emotion and intensity fit reality.",

        "Opposite to Emotion Action": "Acting opposite to the urge that comes with an unjustified emotion. Use this when emotions don't fit the facts and you need to change them. Practice by identifying the action urge, then doing the complete opposite with your body, posture, and behavior.",

        "Problem Solving": "Taking concrete steps to change situations causing justified painful emotions. Use this when the facts support your emotion and the problem is solvable. Practice by defining the problem clearly, brainstorming solutions, choosing one, implementing it, and evaluating results.",

        "Accumulating Positive Emotions (Short Term)": "Doing pleasant activities to build positive feelings in the moment. Use this to improve mood and increase resilience on difficult days. Practice by creating a list of enjoyable activities and doing at least one daily, being mindful during the experience.",

        "Accumulating Positive Emotions (Long Term)": "Building a life worth living by working toward valued goals. Use this to create sustainable happiness and meaning. Practice by identifying your values, setting goals aligned with them, and taking concrete steps toward those goals regularly.",

        "Building Mastery": "Doing activities that make you feel competent and accomplished. Use this to combat depression, build confidence, and increase positive emotion. Practice by engaging in challenging but achievable tasks and gradually increasing difficulty as skills improve.",

        "Cope Ahead": "Rehearsing skills for handling upcoming stressful situations. Use this when you know a difficult event is coming. Practice by imagining the situation in detail, identifying skills to use, mentally rehearsing using them, and relaxing after the imagery.",

        "PLEASE Skills": "Taking care of physical health to reduce vulnerability to negative emotions. Treat Physical illness, balance Eating, avoid mood-Altering substances, ensure Sleep, and Exercise regularly. Use this daily as prevention and when noticing increased emotional reactivity.",

        "Nightmare Protocol": "A specific practice for changing recurring nightmares. Use this when nightmares disrupt sleep and increase distress. Practice by writing down the nightmare, changing the ending while awake, and rehearsing the new version before sleep.",

        "Sleep Hygiene": "Creating conditions and habits that promote quality sleep. Use this when sleep problems increase emotional vulnerability. Practice by maintaining consistent sleep schedules, creating a restful environment, and avoiding stimulants and screens before bed.",

        "Mindfulness of Current Emotions": "Observing emotions without trying to change or push them away. Use this to reduce suffering from emotions about emotions and let feelings naturally shift. Practice by noticing where emotions show up in your body, describing them without judgment, and allowing them to be present.",

        "Managing Extreme Emotions": "Using crisis skills when emotions reach overwhelming intensity. Use this when emotions threaten to lead to harmful actions. Practice by recognizing early warning signs and immediately applying distress tolerance skills before problem behavior occurs.",

        "Troubleshooting ER Skills": "Identifying barriers to emotion regulation and finding solutions. Use this when ER skills aren't working as expected. Practice by checking if you identified emotions correctly, whether facts were checked accurately, and if myths about emotions interfere.",

        // Distress Tolerance
        "STOP Skill": "A four-step crisis skill to avoid impulsive harmful reactions. Stop, Take a step back, Observe, Proceed mindfully. Use this the moment you feel intense urges to act destructively.",

        "Pros and Cons of DT Skills": "Weighing costs and benefits of tolerating distress versus acting on destructive urges. Use this in crisis moments when you're deciding whether to use skills or give in. Practice by thinking through short and long-term consequences of both tolerating and not tolerating distress.",

        "TIP Skills": "Changing body chemistry rapidly to reduce extreme arousal. Use Temperature (cold water on face), Intense exercise, Paced breathing, or Progressive relaxation when emotions are at crisis level. Use this when you need immediate physiological calming.",

        "Distract with Wise Mind ACCEPTS": "Seven distraction strategies to get through crisis moments. Activities, Contributing, Comparisons, opposite Emotions, Pushing away, Thoughts, Sensations. Use this when you need to survive the next few minutes or hours without making things worse.",

        "IMPROVE the Moment": "Making a bad situation more tolerable using seven strategies. Imagery, Meaning, Prayer, Relaxation, One thing in the moment, Vacation, Encouragement. Use this to change your experience of crisis even when you can't change the situation.",

        "Body Scan Meditation": "Systematically bringing awareness through the entire body. Use this to ground yourself, reduce tension, and connect with physical experience. Practice by slowly moving attention from head to toes or toes to head, noticing sensations without judgment.",

        "Sensory Awareness": "Using the five senses to anchor attention in the present moment. Use this when overwhelmed by thoughts or emotions to ground in concrete reality. Practice by deliberately noticing what you see, hear, smell, taste, and touch right now.",

        "Radical Acceptance": "Completely accepting reality as it is without fighting it. Use this when reality is painful but unchangeable and resistance creates suffering. Practice by noticing rejection of reality, turning your mind toward acceptance, and using willing hands and half-smile.",

        "Turning the Mind": "Making an inner choice to accept rather than reject reality. Use this repeatedly when your mind goes back to fighting what is. Practice by noticing rejection, making a conscious decision to accept, and returning to this choice each time resistance arises.",

        "Willingness": "Doing what's needed in each situation without resentment. Use this instead of willfulness when you're stuck fighting reality. Practice by participating completely in what the moment requires, letting go of sitting on your hands or refusing what's effective.",

        "Half-Smiling and Willing Hands": "Using body posture to shift toward acceptance. Use this as a physical practice of acceptance and willingness. Practice by relaxing facial muscles into a slight smile and unclenching hands with palms up or open.",

        "Mindfulness of Current Thoughts": "Observing thoughts as mental events rather than facts. Use this to reduce fusion with thoughts and see thinking as just thinking. Practice by watching thoughts come and go like clouds, labeling them as thoughts, and letting them pass without engaging.",

        // Distress Tolerance - Addiction
        "Reinforcing Non-Addictive Behaviors": "Rewarding yourself for avoiding addictive behaviors and engaging in healthy alternatives. Use this to strengthen recovery by making sobriety more rewarding than use. Practice by identifying meaningful rewards and giving them to yourself immediately after resisting urges or choosing healthy behaviors.",

        "Burning Bridges and Building New Ones": "Removing access to addictive behaviors and creating new, healthy connections. Use this to make relapse harder and recovery easier. Practice by eliminating triggers, ending relationships tied to addiction, and actively developing new people and places that support sobriety.",

        "Alternate Rebellion": "Finding ways to rebel or break rules that don't involve addictive behaviors. Use this when rebelliousness or defiance drives addictive urges. Practice by identifying safe ways to express independence and nonconformity like harmless rule-breaking, unconventional choices, or creative expression.",

        "Adaptive Denial": "Temporarily distracting from thoughts about using without pretending addiction doesn't exist. Use this when cravings or addiction thoughts become overwhelming. Practice by acknowledging the thought briefly then deliberately refocusing attention on other activities without dwelling on or fighting the urge."
    ]
}
